    "minutes": 1.25,
    "title": "Features arrive faster.<br><em>Production readiness falls behind.</em>",
    "notes": `Talk track (about 75 seconds):
Now consider the work needed to make those features safe to release and reliable to operate. Watch the fast implementation wheel on the left. Features arrive faster, but acceptance tests, assurance checks, and release-readiness work still follow the old cycle on the right.
Much of that work receives its input after implementation. Feature volume increases; readiness capacity and timing do not change with it. The backlog grows between the two loops.
The slow wheel is working, not stopped. One feature enters it, but more arrive than the process can absorb. Features wait. Customer value waits.
Follow the return line. A late finding can require changes to code already built and to work that depends on it. The issue is not that these teams should work harder or skip checks. The volume and timing of their work no longer fit the rate of feature delivery.
More finished code. More unfinished readiness work. We now have three gaps to address: the wrong outcome, unchecked assumptions, and work that cannot yet reach production.
Presenter guidance:
Stay in the problem section. The cause is a delivery model that leaves too much readiness work until after implementation. AI-assisted development can increase incoming feature volume without increasing the capacity of acceptance, assurance, and release-readiness work. The backlog grows when arrival rate exceeds readiness capacity. This is a conditional capacity gap, not an inevitable result of AI or a claim that every organization works this way.
The right wheel names work, not teams. Acceptance tests check intended behaviour, integration, and user acceptance. Assurance checks assess relevant quality, security, privacy, compliance, and reliability risks. Release readiness covers deployment, access, monitoring, rollback, documentation, and support ownership. These are examples of work, not three required serial gates or a demand that every feature receive the same review. The wheel is still turning: the problem is late work and an incoming load it cannot absorb, not idle or unwilling specialists. This risk pattern does not imply that tests and reviews belong only after build.
The fast wheel sends eight conceptual feature markers into the queue. One enters the readiness process, leaving seven waiting at the final freeze. Markers, speeds, and the six-second sequence are not measured throughput, risk scores, released features, or accepted customer outcomes. Unlike slide 7, these markers represent separate pieces of work, not copies of one business assumption. The return marker represents a possible late finding and its correction, not every reviewed feature failing.
The animation completes near the start of the talk track. Use the final backlog and return line to explain the impact. Replay motion restarts the sequence. Pause motion freezes its active time budget. Hidden pages and deck pause preserve that budget; slide exit stops the sequence. Overview, reduced motion, print, and script failure show the complete static meaning.
If useful, ask leaders to consider tens or hundreds of features built in days. Treat this as a planning scenario, not a measured claim or a forecast. Increased volume can expose readiness work that a longer build cycle previously hid. A late finding can cause redesign, repeat checks, and changes to dependent work. Faster building alone does not prove faster safe release or useful customer value.
Leave the response for the next section. The later response is to shift suitable work left and do it continuously, including security-agent support during design, planning, and coding. Prepared foundations help, but they do not replace continuous evidence, specialist judgment, operational ownership, or required independent review. Do not recommend removing assurance, slowing engineering, making every decision upfront, or simply adding people to a central end-of-cycle queue. AI output is not its own proof.
Next, move from faster code delivery to better business outcomes through changes to the operating model and four pillars.`,
