    "title": "Hypervelocity Engineering<br><em>How we change the delivery operating model</em>",
    "notes": `Talk track (about 30 seconds):
Hypervelocity Engineering changes how we work, not just how quickly we code. Start with serial handoffs. Now bring the customer and expert crew into a short feedback loop: shape the idea, validate the need, engineer an increment, and learn what comes next. Align the supporting delivery work to that loop. Keep assurance continuous where suitable and independent review where required. The four pillars show how.
Presenter guidance:
Start with the serial flow. Advance once with Right Arrow or Space to replace it with idea, validation, engineering, and feedback around the customer and expert crew. Backward navigation restores the serial view. Overview, reduced motion, print, and script failure show the labelled side-by-side comparison.
The circle represents a short learning and decision cycle, not a completed release on each turn. Four fixed purple arrowheads at the top, right, bottom, and left show the clockwise sequence. The circle does not animate or imply a measured rate. The customer and expert crew stay readable at the centre.
Product and design, engineering and testing, security and operations, and release and service work support that loop. These are examples of delivery functions, not four serial gates, a fixed team roster, or one-to-one ownership of the four stages. Keep suitable work close to the learning cycle so feedback can change the next increment.
Connect this change to slides 6-8: validate the customer problem, keep business decisions connected to evidence, and perform suitable readiness work continuously rather than leaving it all until after build. The assurance line is intentionally absent from the visual. Required independent review, separation of duties, specialist judgment, and operational ownership remain.
The goal is the right customer outcome and an engineered solution that can be operated, not maximum code output. This is an operating-model proposal to test and adapt, not a measured result or a mandated formula. Applying rapid co-development practices to full production delivery requires additional operational and assurance work; a POC or MVP is not a production-ready release. Shared ownership does not require constant customer availability or make every participant an approval gate.
Keep toolkit logos, commands, and detailed pillar explanations off this slide. The next slide maps the opening constraints to the four practices.`,
